WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:05.560
(Laughing) Look at how fast Spiderman is rendering.

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:08.840
Okay, wait, yeah, we'll put this in. Okay.

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:16.180
What's up everybody, I'm Peter and as you may remember I am working on my very own Spiderman short film, which is

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:19.500
thankfully almost done. Let me show you something.

00:00:19.529 --> 00:00:21.719
This is kind of the place where I sleep at night

00:00:21.939 --> 00:00:27.179
Master bed and little trick that I learned which makes it a lot better is you can take these little flaps

00:00:27.359 --> 00:00:30.299
and now you got more leg space. I've got a pillow right here.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:35.160
The only thing that kind of sucks is that there's not enough room to spread out and so you'll always

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:37.200
wake up with one arm asleep.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.680
Here's the VFX spreadsheet. All of the green is shots I've completed. Black is shots I've cut out.

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:46.400
Look at all this completion. Almost done, baby. Final stretch.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:51.030
I have about 20 visual effects shots left to do excluding just some green screen stuff

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:54.960
which I can pretty much automate so I have three days to do...

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:57.120
...two, three, four, five, six, seven, eight, nine, ten, eleven...

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:04.000
23 shots, which if you ask me is pretty manageable because these aren't full CGI shots. I have finished all of those, thank goodness.

00:01:04.019 --> 00:01:07.679
And now these are the kind of lower caliber VFX shots that I have left.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.709
I'll send them off to Wren, see if he can take care of them.

00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:16.140
So now I just have 22 low caliber VFX shots left to do and then I will send it off for music and sound, and

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:18.299
it will be all done.

00:01:18.299 --> 00:01:24.029
Going into this project I was like, "Sure, I'll have 150 VFX shots, many of which include character animation, something

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:29.610
I've never done before." Even as I was thinking that, in the back of my mind somewhere I was like, "There's no f***ing way

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:35.339
I'm gonna do this. This is an insane amount of work and it's really dumb for me to set myself up to...

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:43.920
fail like this." Yeah. Laughably, I thought I would have this done by summer 2018

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:49.120
(Laughter/dying)

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:52.560
Before I even got to Corridor I thought, "Oh, I'll edit it on my Surface Pro."

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:56.560
(More laugh dying)

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:01.020
Now that I have these very nice computers to work with, I have... Let me calculate the hours

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:05.909
I've spent working on this. Sixty days at ten to twelve hours each. Almost 700 hours

00:02:06.009 --> 00:02:10.979
I've spent on post-production and it's barely enough to finish.

00:02:10.979 --> 00:02:14.398
I'm literally getting by on the skin of my teeth here

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:21.780
I have not learned a good lesson about managing expectations throughout this project because I had unrealistic expectations and...

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:25.040
it all worked out, so...

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:29.549
I guess I'll just keep doing that, keep paying the price for it.

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:32.319
I love Spiderman.

00:02:32.319 --> 00:02:34.060
I'll always love Spiderman.

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:39.839
We're here today in the Corridor Digital studios. Studio? Maybe it's... maybe it's plural, maybe it's singular.

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:44.610
I don't know. Point is we're here to talk about Foam Fortnite. Foam blasters, Fortnite, together.

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:50.070
We did Nerf Team Fortress. We did Nerf John Wick. Foam Fortnite seemed like a natural evolution in that process,

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:51.180
so here we are.

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:58.500
Clint has created a very competent script. Jarrett and Christian and myself are here to figure out exactly how to go about this because we have

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:02.639
two weeks from tomorrow to figure everything out for this video.

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:09.620
So that location. There is a town outside of Yosemite called Yosemite Valley. Yeah, that's generally the area.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:14.900
[Clint] Outside of other concerns, I think this is perfect because it's green, you got your Christmas trees, you got your grizzmies.

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:17.669
You even have rivers, maybe a lake,

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:22.530
it looks like? So I think this hits a lot of the aesthetic that you get with Fortnite, you know, it's very green

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:27.040
It's very vivid. You got your blues, you got your greens. Got your crispy trees.

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:34.400
It's pretty much the easiest, cleanest way to go production-wise. So it's five and a half hours by car, which is totally drivable in an afternoon.

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:36.170
So, so let's talk about this.

00:03:36.170 --> 00:03:42.049
How many days of production do you think we need to cover the main part up to the birthday party?

00:03:42.049 --> 00:03:43.080
I think I can do it in two.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:45.000
[Jake] Well, yeah, but... how many days...

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:45.980
[Clint] I planned for two.

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:46.740
[Jake] ...are best?

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:50.480
Always more. Always more days are best. Always more days are best.

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:54.769
I mean if we got three days, dude, we can make this look mmm. Like real nice, take our time on this, you know.

00:03:54.769 --> 00:03:59.419
We can have two, three takes. We can do three takes. We got action, right?

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:02.839
We got running, we got gunning. We got some walls we gotta throw up.

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:06.140
We have some VFX plays we gotta get. We got some acting moments to hit.

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:09.649
There's a few more layers to this script than usual.

00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:16.130
We need a day before we start photography to just plan and scout and get everything in order.

00:04:16.229 --> 00:04:19.609
So that's a five day total plus a day travel back.

00:04:19.769 --> 00:04:25.638
So you're talking about a six day time frame.

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:31.480
Jarrett, basically, we got to have those done by the end of the week.

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:35.940
Hey, we're making some sweet, sweet Foam Fortnite weapons.

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:41.840
Fortnite is very cartoony. When a designer for a video game is drawing these things the sky's the limit, right?

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:44.640
So they just do all these shenanigans

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:50.480
and throw weird fun designs on there, and it doesn't matter because it's not real and it can just be there.

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:53.480
You know we could build this from foam from scratch.

00:04:53.480 --> 00:04:55.760
We could 3D print things. We could do a lot.

00:04:55.760 --> 00:05:00.360
But we don't have the time or the budget for that. To manage the expectations of our creator

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:03.960
You should go big, right. When you're when you're first coming down with your idea

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:08.440
be as big as possible and then start cutting back to reality. Say, "Well we can't do that,

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:13.000
we can't quite do that. Well we can do this, but we can sort of make things into real life."

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:18.680
So this, we grabbed... this is a G36. This is the sub-machine gun.

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:24.400
So we just took one that we had, I took some of the parts off of it, gave it the paint job and then since

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:27.120
this guy has three barrels

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:32.520
I've made these, which I'm going to put on here, and then I did a third

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:36.160
to glue onto there to match. So it's gonna be like this.

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:38.480
So this is the AK that's in the game

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:43.560
So then I made this boy. So you can see it's similar, still cartoony, but real.

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:47.600
Bringing cartoons into reality poses some challenges because you're like, "Well, what do these parts do?"

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:53.280
Who knows? It doesn't matter, it just needs to look like something. So does it look cool in the video? That's all I care about.

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:56.880
I think I can get the rest of this done today. It's a matter of managing time and then next week

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:00.680
there's a lot of build of set pieces. Could go faster than expected,

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:03.720
so I might have some more time, but the goal is to have this done tomorrow night.

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:06.920
And if that means I stay late, it means I stay late, and we get it done.

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:11.700
This shot right here is definitely one of the beefiest shots in the entire short.

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:17.780
There are so many buildings, random objects in the city, people walking around, cars with all the reflections, the street.

00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:21.160
Everything is in this shot and this computer handles it beautifully.

00:06:21.160 --> 00:06:25.120
So much so that I can see the final rendered version as I work.

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:27.400
Here's what it looks like without rendering, and this is

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:32.740
pretty much what it'll look like once it's finished. And the fact that I can see that while I work is

00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:35.980
insanely helpful for having speedy workflow and being efficient.

00:06:35.980 --> 00:06:39.560
Before I moved my post-production work to Corridor I was working off of a laptop.

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:41.420
(Even more death laugh)

00:06:41.420 --> 00:06:43.040
For something like this it was just not cutting it.

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:49.200
There was no way I was going to be able to do big fancy shots with just a laptop. Puget has been so huge

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:54.080
in helping me make this project. Look at how fast this computer is rendering Spiderman. Damn.

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:55.740
[Carmichael] Like, is that not normal?

00:06:55.740 --> 00:06:56.580
[Peter] No!

00:06:56.580 --> 00:07:00.240
Yeah, look how fast it can render how thick Spiderman is.

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:06.880
Dude, oh my god, look at that. Do you think a Surface Pro could render that? Uh-uh.

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:12.240
Guys, you only have five more days to buy this shirt. It's limited edition, that means once we stop selling it it's gone forever.

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:18.740
We're going to make as many as are ordered. So, five days left to get your order in. Act now, don't delay. Get it!

